### Methods: Statistical Analysis (CONSORT Compliant)

Continuous baseline variables were expressed as mean ± standard deviation (SD) for normally distributed data or median and interquartile range (IQR, 25th to 75th percentiles) for skewed data. Categorical variables were reported as frequencies and percentages. Covariate balance between the intervention group (N = {{ n_intervention | default(0) }}) and control group (N = {{ n_control | default(0) }}) was assessed using Standardized Mean Differences (SMD) according to Austin (2009); an absolute SMD < 0.10 was defined a priori as reflecting negligible imbalance.

The primary efficacy endpoint was analyzed on an Intention-to-Treat (ITT) basis. Cumulative event probabilities were estimated using the Kaplan-Meier method and compared between treatment arms with the two-sided log-rank test. Hazard ratios (HRs) and corresponding 95% confidence intervals (CIs) were computed using Cox proportional hazards regression models with Efron's method for tie handling. The proportional hazards assumption was assessed using Schoenfeld residuals and Grambsch-Therneau tests. Missing baseline covariates were addressed through Multiple Imputation by Chained Equations (MICE) using {{ m_imputations | default(20) }} imputed datasets, with pooling performed under Rubin's rules using Barnard-Rubin (1999) small-sample adjusted degrees of freedom. All statistical tests were two-sided, with a P value < 0.05 considered statistically significant. Analyses were conducted using StatioMed AI / Python lifelines and statsmodels packages.
